# Virtual-Chris Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Lens:** stakeholder lattice, initiative adjacencies, cross-disciplinary pattern matches, and the strategic openings the thesis creates that nobody put on the agenda. This brief is deliberately connective, not adversarial. Other agents will inventory the risks; my job is to hand the Strategist the alliances that turn "MWAA should" into "MWAA can, next quarter, using work already paid for."

The situation, read plainly: MWAA has already procured or announced almost every ingredient a quiet-by-design standard would need. HOK is under contract for 2.8 million square feet of new Dulles concourse design that explicitly promises "thoughtful choices in lighting, acoustics and materials that create a calming environment." United Airlines is spending $500 million on a Concourse E lounge with "cutting-edge soundproofing" and dedicated quiet zones. The Sunflower hidden-disabilities program launched at both airports in 2023 with training extended to airlines and concessionaires. What is missing is not investment. What is missing is a governing document that lets these separate acts of taste and generosity add up to a standard.

That is a very different problem than the one the thesis appears to name. It is not "should MWAA adopt a quiet standard" — it is "who writes it down before the design work already underway hardens without it."

---

## 1. The Lattice — connections the thesis activates

1. **HOK's Tier 2/3 design contract ↔ MWAA Engineering ↔ the 15-year Airline Use Agreement.** HOK's March 2025 selection covers concourses, a new federal inspection facility, and a replacement central utility plant. Acoustic performance criteria added now, before schematic design pricing hardens at the 30–60% level, cost pennies on the dollar of the same criteria added at commissioning. The Use Agreement's majority-in-interest process is the mechanism to bring United along; United is already leaning in through the lounge spec. The window is measured in months, not years.

2. **The Sunflower Program ↔ acoustics ↔ MWAA Communications.** MWAA joined Sunflower in July 2023 and trained employees, airlines, and concessionaires. The program identifies passengers who need lower-stimulation environments but does nothing about the environment itself. A quiet-by-design standard is the physical infrastructure that makes the lanyard mean something operationally rather than symbolically. Same stakeholder base, no new coalition required.

3. **NFPA 72 (2025 ed.) intelligibility floors ↔ Director of Public Safety ↔ acoustic ambient targets.** NFPA 72 requires emergency voice systems to achieve an average Speech Transmission Index of 0.50, with at least 90% of measurement locations at 0.45 or above in each Acoustically Distinguishable Space. The way to guarantee that is to lower ambient noise and PA overlap. A quiet standard is not in tension with the fire code; it is the least expensive way to comply with it. That reframes the Public Safety chair from a skeptic to a co-owner.

4. **The Airport Terminal Program's FY 2026 final round (~$1B) ↔ MWAA capital planning ↔ Concourse E and Tier 2/3 asks.** The December 8, 2025 NOFO for ATP FY26 is the final ATP round under the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act. Terminal PA, FIDS, and acoustic scopes fall inside "airport passenger terminal building" eligibility. A quiet standard converts a passenger-experience talking point into a scored, fundable line item at 80% federal share. Miss this round and the next comparable federal terminal envelope does not yet exist.

5. **DCA's historic architecture ↔ preservation stakeholders ↔ performance-based specs.** Terminal 1 (the 1941 Bolling building) sits inside a Historic District. The Pelli Terminal 2 has its own architectural intent. A prescriptive standard that names materials or hardware would fight preservation. A performance-based standard (reverberation time, background noise criteria, STI at defined receiver points) is preservation-neutral — it tells the designer *what to achieve*, not *what to install*. Preservation advocates become allies rather than obstacles.

6. **TSA checkpoint zoning ↔ TSA Cares ↔ Sunflower.** TSA already owns queue and announcement content at the checkpoint and already administers TSA Cares. Every quiet-airport program that has worked in the US (SFO, EWR Terminal A) had TSA in the room from the start. TSA's incentives point the same direction: fewer overlapping announcements make required security messages more intelligible, which is the metric TSA itself is graded on.

7. **United's $22.5B+ Dulles announcement ↔ hold-room acoustic spec ↔ airline commercial strategy.** United has already agreed, in effect, to a quiet lounge. The one-hop question is whether the same acoustic thinking extends to the hold rooms United controls under the Use Agreement. Framed as a joint MWAA–United standard, it is a photo opportunity attached to the largest airline capital commitment at IAD in a generation. Framed as an imposition, it is a fight.

8. **ACAA 2024 rulemaking ↔ DOT ↔ multi-channel redundancy.** The Air Carrier Access Act amendments on accessible communication effectively require the redundant visual, digital, and staffed channels that a quiet-by-design standard also requires. MWAA is going to build those channels anyway. Writing a single MWAA standard that satisfies both ACAA and the quiet thesis is one document instead of two — and it puts DOT's Office of Aviation Consumer Protection on the same side of the table as MWAA Engineering, which is not the usual seating chart.

