## 2. Three Pattern Matches

### 2.1 The hospital "quiet at night" moment — HCAHPS, 2007–2015

When CMS added "quietness of the hospital environment" to the HCAHPS survey in 2007, it exposed a durable industry-wide low score. Hospitals responded not with silence but with governance: sound-absorbing ceiling tile, quieter door latches, pagers on vibrate, phones on lower ring volume, rounds moved inside patient rooms rather than in the corridor, and — critically — a designated owner for the metric. Cleveland Clinic reported a six-percentage-point HCAHPS improvement on "always quiet at night" in two of four participating units within months of standing the program up. Johns Hopkins invested in soundproofing and reconfigured nurse stations to the same effect.

**The mapping is precise.** MWAA's analogue to HCAHPS is the ACI Airport Service Quality (ASQ) survey. ASQ's "atmosphere of the airport" and "walking distance / wayfinding" scores are the terminal equivalent of "always quiet at night." What moved the HCAHPS score was not a technology purchase; it was a written standard with a named owner, a monitoring instrument, and behavior change across a coalition of clinicians, facility engineers, and vendors who did not previously coordinate. The MWAA quiet standard should be built the way Cleveland Clinic built theirs: as a governance intervention with acoustic instrumentation, not as an acoustics project with a governance appendix. The hospital pattern also debunks the loudest counterargument: HCAHPS quietness improved *without* reductions in life-safety intelligibility, because the interventions were zoned by area and time-of-day rather than uniform.

### 2.2 The sterile cockpit rule — 14 CFR §121.542, 1981

The FAA promulgated 14 CFR §121.542 in 1981 in response to accidents (Eastern 212 at Charlotte in 1974 is the classic reference) in which non-essential cockpit conversation contributed to crew error during critical phases of flight. The rule is deceptively short: below 10,000 feet, and on the ground during taxi and takeoff, no non-essential conversation, no non-essential activity. It did not silence the cockpit. It defined a *critical phase* in which the signal-to-noise ratio had to be high.

**The mapping.** For a passenger, the terminal has its own critical phases: the last twenty minutes before boarding, the seven minutes at a checkpoint, the moment of a gate change during irregular operations. The sterile-cockpit lesson is that intelligibility is not an average across the day — it is a variable that must be defended during specific windows and can be relaxed at other times. The quiet standard that will actually work at IAD and DCA is one that specifies *when* and *where* the signal-to-noise ratio must be high, not one that specifies a uniform decibel target. The regulatory precedent inside aviation is unusually clean. A skeptical airport executive who dismisses "quiet airport" as European aesthetics has already accepted the sterile-cockpit rule for forty-five years. It is the same logic applied one hundred feet closer to the passenger.

### 2.3 The library zoning transition — Carnegie hush to modern zoned space

American libraries spent a century enforcing uniform silence and then, roughly between the mid-1990s and the mid-2010s, transitioned to zoned acoustic environments: silent reading rooms, group study rooms, cafés, maker spaces. The transition survived intense professional debate because it was framed not as abandoning silence but as *reserving* it for places where readers actually needed it, and providing alternative environments for uses that were previously suppressed or driven out.

**The mapping.** The failure mode of "quiet airports" is exactly the failure mode librarians warned about: uniform silence pushes activity into hallways, drives out families with children, and creates a class of user (the neurodivergent, the anxious infrequent traveler, the parent) who reads the environment as hostile. The library answer — zoned space with explicit signage, staff who explain the zones, and an accessible alternative for every restricted use — is the same answer a terminal needs. It also explains why "silent airport" as a brand fails: it names an aesthetic rather than a service design. A well-zoned terminal reads as generous, not austere.

